    "text_856865393": {
      "jcr:primaryType": "nt:unstructured",
      "jcr:createdBy": "hannah.white@stryker.com",
      "jcr:title": "Technology",
      "cq:masterBuildingBlock": true,
      "jcr:lastModifiedBy": "hannah.white@stryker.com",
      "jcr:created": "Thu Jun 11 2026 16:27:22 GMT+0000",
      "jcr:lastModified": "Thu Jun 11 2026 16:33:36 GMT+0000",
      "sling:resourceType": "cq/experience-fragments/editor/components/buildingblock",
      "text_856865393214941576173466": {
        "jcr:primaryType": "nt:unstructured",
        "jcr:lastModifiedBy": "hannah.white@stryker.com",
        "text": "<p><span class=\"futura-bold\">Technology plays an important part in Cleveland Clinic’s OR safety efforts.</span> Surgical teams use an EMR module called OpTime, which makes charting seamless. And adjunct technology, such as Stryker’s SurgiCount+, enables nursing teams and surgical techs to scan sponges at the beginning and end of a procedure to ensure no sponges are left behind, which happens as often as 11 times a day, every day, across the U.S.<sup>1</sup></p>\n<p>Caregivers and patients in the OR may also be exposed to dangerous splashes and spills, bacterial and viral particulates, and airborne chemicals and pollutants. Stryker’s Neptune is a constantly closed waste management solution with integrated HEPA and ULPA filters that protect the air that clinicians and patients breathe in the OR. And Stryker’s disposable QuickWick mats plug into the Neptune Waste Management System to keep the floor of the OR and procedural spaces dry.</p>\n<p>Health systems like Cleveland Clinic know they must leverage a portfolio of tools and techniques to achieve zero harm in the operating room. “Our end goal is to ensure all patients get the surgical care that they need and that both patients and caregivers are safe,” concludes Salamon.</p>\n",
        "jcr:lastModified": "Thu Jun 11 2026 16:30:31 GMT+0000",
        "sling:resourceType": "stryker/components/content/text",
        "textIsRich": "true",
